y  caused by the combined effect of more  than 50 sleeping pills found partly  digested in her stomach  the presence of the sleeping pills was  unexpected but it was also something of  a lifeline for the investigation  they still had no idea who the woman was  but they were now pretty sure who'd  killed her because if the isle woman had  died with a belly full of prescription  drugs  that probably meant only one thing  suicide as you can probably imagine  plenty of people were unhappy with that  verdict  suicide by self-immolation is  practically unheard of and on the rare  occasions it does happen it's almost  invariably carried out somewhere very  public in order to make a political  statement  without putting too fine a point on it  it's an absolutely horrific way to go  not one many people would willingly  choose  even if they did dose up on sleeping  pills beforehand  even so the istal woman's death was  officially deemed a suicide and the case  was closed in early 1971  just months after the body had first  been discovered  in the decades since literally thousands  of journalists amateur sleuths and crime  writers have poured over the details of  the isle woman's death searching for new  clues and coming up with their own  theories as to who she was and what  really happened on that desolate  mountainside near bergen in 1970 the  most popular theory is that the isdal  woman was a spy of some kind probably  for the soviets or maybe the israelis  considering what i've told you about the  case so far i'm going to go ahead and  guess you might have seen that one  coming  the fake identities the outfits that  boarded on disguises the strange  behavior in hotels moving from room to  room  it's a pretty compelling theory and it  turns out there's actually even more  evidence to back it up because whilst it  was never publicly acknowledged at the  time the official investigation into the  istal woman's death also explored the  idea she may have been a spy the cold  wa