's most famous  spy catcher  and they don't seem to be able to agree  on the likelihood that the mysterious  woman was a foreign agent  that might come as a bit of a surprise  but when you think about it some of her  actions weren't actually very spy-like  at all  take her apparent meticulousness and  sniffing the labels out of her clothes  and removing them from all her other  possessions  that might sound like spy school 101  stuff  but is it really after all you'd expect  any spy worth his soul to simply wear  clothes that matched his cover story the  isdal woman was a foreigner in norway  she wasn't trying to pass herself off as  norwegian  she could have worn clothes from pretty  much anywhere without anyone butting an  eyelid and the same goes for her  cosmetics and the other items she  carried  you could argue that removing all the  labels was far more suspicious than a  belgian woman owning belgium face cream  and wearing belgian clothes then there  were her multiple aliases in both bergen  and stavenger the istel woman used  different identities in different hotels  within the same city just a few days  apart which seems like an entirely  unnecessary risk for an undercover  operative to take  these aren't particularly big cities  even today and people working in the  hotel trade often have contacts in other  hotels  what if someone had realized that the  same stylish foreigner was checking into  different hotels using different names  all over the city  what isn't entirely clear is whether the  israel woman had official documents to  back up her identities  none were ever found and it may just be  that she dreamt the aliases up all by  herself  the forms were filled out in her own  handwriting after all so there's no  guarantee she was asked to provide her  passport to hotel staff there's no  denying that the isdal woman's  activities were strange but whether or  not they match those of a professional  spy  is debatable but if not a spy then what  could possibly explain the strange  details of the case some people believe  the is still a woman may have been some  kind of high level escort or prostitute  that would fit with the hotel visits and  perhaps even the different identities  she was seen with various men including  important ones and the wigs and other  outfits found in her suitcases may have  been for the benefit of clients with  differing tastes you could even argue  that the resistance detectives met  during the investigation  came from one of her powerful clients  who was keen to keep his naughty secrets  under wraps  others think the isdal woman might have  been involved in organized crime or was  perhaps an international art thief  the problem with most of these alternate  theories is that there's no hard  evidence to back them up  there's also the issue that none of the  theories i've mentioned so far including  the spy theory explain the incredibly  strange circumstances of the isle  woman's death  most people discount the official  version of events that the isthil woman  committed suicide partly because of the  way she died  as i've said self-immolation is an  incredibly rare way to kill yourself but  something that's also often overlooked  in this case is the fact that it's also  a pretty bloody weird way to murder  someone  i'm no hitman  obviously but i'm guessing that forcing  someone to swallow more than 50 sleeping  pills against their will is probably  pretty tricky  and even if you could do it  why bother the obvious answer to that  question is to make a murder look like a  suicide but if that was the aim  why set the victim on fire afterwards  if the isdi woman was a spy why was she  just killed in such a strange way and  then just left on a mountainside where  someone was bound to find her sooner or  later  it's thought that there was almost a  week between her death and the discovery  of her body  more than enough time to dispose of the  evidence  the truth is for now nobody has really  managed to come up with compelling  answers to these questions  and none of the theories so far proposed  seem capable of accounting for all the  cases unusual details  it isn't completely crazy to suggest  that the conclusion the official  investigation reached more than 50 years  ago that the isle woman committed  suicide does a good job as any theory of  explaining the evidence  but perhaps the details of this case are  so insane because only insanity itself  can actually account for them  most people look at the istal woman's  actions the fastidious removal of all  identifying information from her  possessions  the multiple identities the odd habit of  changing hotel rooms and think spy  but you don't have to squint all that  hard to see those same actions through  the lens of mental illness was the isle  woman simply a disturbed individual  paranoid and alone  maybe by taking sleeping pills then  dousing herself in petrol and lighting a  match  she was simply eliminating one last  piece of the fractured identity she so  desperately wanted to hide  much like all the other theories it's  hard to argue that this one fits all the  facts either the isdal woman clearly had  money means and the wherewithal to take  herself around norway and europe doing  whatever it was she was doing  then there are the eyewitness accounts  placing her in conversation with various  important people it's hard to see how  they might have happened if she was  living out some grand delusion  if you're finding it hard to know what  the hell to believe right now i don't  blame you it's almost as though there's  some key piece of evidence missing from  the case some unifying clue that if  found would give new meaning to the  confusing details of the story  that just don't seem to align no matter  how you choose to jiggle them around  as for whether that clue will ever be  found well there's every reason to hope  so  the isdal woman's case may be more than  50 years old but it was officially  reopened back in 2016  and every so often new evidence comes to  light much of that evidence centers on  the one piece of the isle woman's body  that remained above ground after her  burial in 1971. her jawbone  the jawbone is significant because along  with her fingerprints it represents a  second piece of identifying information  the fire didn't destroy  the is the woman had some seriously  weird looking dental work done including  14 fillings several gold crowns and a  solid gold tooth  back in the 70s it was hoped a dentist  might recognize his handiwork in a  deceased woman's jaw and come forward  but nobody ever did  today it's hoped that the jawbone along  with the tissue samples taken during the  ista woman's autopsy  might be used to unlock the secrets of  her identity at its most fundamental  level using her genetic information and  other data locked away in her remains  analysis has so far revealed that she  was most likely born in norenburg  germany sometime around 1930 and that  she probably moved to the france-germany  border as a child in 2019 the dna doe  project a pioneering forensic genealogy  not-for-profit showed an interest in  taking on the case the project which  launched in 2017 has already helped  identify a remarkable number of unknown  people in cases fought to be essentially  unsolvable  by tracking down their living relatives  could the istal woman be next  let's hope so  thanks for watching
 

